## Coverage

29 cases before (11 close-wrapper + 18 hook). 11 died with their subject; 18
were extracted; 2 were added at the verify gate's request to pin the hook's
reworded BLOCKED message. **20 pass, 0 fail.**

Four negative controls were run and reverted, each proving a different thing:

| Control | Result |
|---|---|
| break `refs/heads/*` classification | 5/18 fail — the suite drives the real hook |
| hostile `bd` (exit 7) | 13/18 fail — the carried PATH shim is load-bearing |
| blank the BLOCKED message | presence assertion fails |
| re-add `dogfood-close.sh` to the message | absence assertion fails |

The first control replaced one the plan specified that did NOT fail; the
diagnosis (the breakage is masked by a downstream `_has_code_ref` guard) is
recorded in the verify artifact rather than the control quietly swapped.

## What the gates cost, and what they bought

Eleven rounds across four gates; five did not pass.

- **plan r1, r2** — self-granted exemptions to the acceptance, a test file read
  as one suite when it is three, an extraction dependency list missing the bd
  shim, and a verification grep anchored on `.sh` that structurally could not
  see a live wrong pointer.
- **verify r1, r3** — the artifact claimed the edited hook had been exercised by
  pushes (false: `core.hooksPath` resolves to the main checkout, whose copy is
  unedited), then, after correction, still opened by asserting the thing it
  retracted three paragraphs later.
- **docs r1, r2** — a replacement pointer attributing an error to `etude gate`
  that only `capture-gate` can emit, then a hook comment claiming
  `etude capture` / `etude gate` push refs when neither pushes anything.

**Every one of the five was a false or incomplete CLAIM, not defective code.**
That is worth stating plainly: a bead whose entire purpose is removing
documentation that describes a mechanism the repo no longer has spent five
rounds writing documentation that described mechanisms the repo does not have.
The sweep was disciplined about finding stale pointers and careless about
whether each replacement was true — which is the same failure one level down.
Docs r2 is the sharpest instance: the original comment was stale but TRUE, and
the rewrite made it false.

Two rounds also produced work beyond corrections:

- The `review-gate-runbook.md` section was stale on two counts nobody had
  flagged — it asserted docs is not a captured stage and prescribed the
  transport-only alias `etude-1od` had removed two beads earlier. A live
  judgement runbook was instructing readers to do the thing this epic had just
  eliminated.
- Docs r2 exposed an operational gap: the BLOCKED message told an operator to
  capture and gate, but the audit it fronts GAPs on `unpushed-ref`, so following
  the message exactly left them still blocked. The message now names the push
  step, and the supervisor runbook's close checklist names `etude sync` so the
  loop closes where the message points.

## Honest limits

- **The edited hook is not the executing hook from here.** `core.hooksPath`
  points at the main checkout's `.beads/hooks`; this worktree's copy becomes
  live only once this lands and that copy is updated. Nothing detects drift
  between them — filed as `etude-6d9`.
- **Nothing runs the shell suites automatically.** No CI exists, and `make test`
  is `go test ./...`. `shell-test` gives them one command, not enforcement.
- **`make dogfood-audit` does not pass**, on pre-existing `cadence-sidecar` debt
  bead `.4` will remove. No count is quoted: it scans a moving `--last 9`
  window, and two earlier versions of that claim went stale before a seat could
  check them.

